  ADVISORY LOCK

  Fixed namespace: 728341001 (const backfillAccountingAdvisoryLockNamespace int32 = 728341001)
  Schema identity source: SELECT current_schema() on the acquired lock connection, then SELECT hashtext($1) on the schema name. current_schema() returns the first existing schema on the connection's search_path.
  Pool/schema consistency: CONFIRMED — newTestSchema applies SET search_path TO <schema> via pgxpool.Config.AfterConnect, which fires on every physical connection the pool ever opens, including the one acquired for the advisory lock. The lock connection and all query connections resolve unqualified names against the same schema; no lock-schema vs. query-schema mismatch is possible. Production pool uses the same search_path from the connection string URL runtime parameter (baked in by testDBURLWithSchema's q.Set("search_path", schemaName) in CLI tests, matching the production store.Connect path).
  Same-schema exclusion: CONFIRMED — TestBackfillAccounting_ConcurrentRunRejected: test pre-holds the advisory lock on a manually-acquired connection from the same pool, then calls BackfillAccounting on the same store → ErrBackfillAlreadyRunning. Deterministic (no sleep); the lock is held before BackfillAccounting is ever called.
  Different-schema independence: CONFIRMED — TestBackfillAccounting_DifferentSchemaNotBlocked: schema A's advisory lock held; schema B's BackfillAccounting succeeds. Test asserts schemaNameA != schemaNameB and schemaKeyA != schemaKeyB as fixture preconditions; fails with "fixture bug" on collision rather than silently giving a false positive.
  NULL/empty schema fail-closed: CONFIRMED — schemaScopedLockKey scans current_schema() into *string, checks name == nil || *name == "" → ErrSchemaIdentityUnavailable. No fallback to any database-global key, no random key, no public-schema assumption.
  Unlock same key: CONFIRMED — defer captures namespace, schemaKey from schemaScopedLockKey at acquisition time and passes them verbatim to pg_advisory_unlock($1, $2). No recomputation against possibly-changed session state.
  Session-loss release: CONFIRMED — pg_try_advisory_lock is session-level (not pg_try_advisory_xact_lock). PostgreSQL releases session-level advisory locks automatically when the holding connection closes or crashes. The comment in BackfillAccounting explicitly notes this: "pg_try_advisory_lock is session-scoped, not held open-endedly by a leaked reference."
  hashtext collision possible: YES — hashtext() returns int32 (2^32 possible values). Two different schema names can produce the same value. Probability per pair: ≈ 2.3×10⁻¹⁰.
  hashtext collision consequence: FALSE CONTENTION only (availability failure: a backfill against schema B returns ErrBackfillAlreadyRunning when schema A's unrelated backfill is running). Cannot produce false accounting (the colliding backfill writes zero rows — it fails entirely). Cannot produce failure to exclude same-schema concurrent writers (same schema → same hash → correct exclusion; this direction always works). The test explicitly guards against this by failing as a "fixture bug" if two test schemas produce the same key.
  Schema OID preferable?: Schema OID (pg_catalog.pg_namespace.oid) would eliminate the collision risk entirely (OIDs are unique per schema) and avoid the extra hashtext() query step. However, it requires a catalog query dependency and the current collision risk is orders of magnitude below any operational concern. Not a blocking issue.
  Current design acceptable: YES — collision creates only a rare operational nuisance (spurious ErrBackfillAlreadyRunning), never false monetary data. For this project's stated correctness requirements (no false rows), the design is safe. No change required.

